diff --git a/JGDMS_COMPATIBILITY.md b/JGDMS_COMPATIBILITY.md
index 0f9804817..04bd35dba 100644
--- a/JGDMS_COMPATIBILITY.md
+++ b/JGDMS_COMPATIBILITY.md
@@ -14,6 +14,35 @@ ## Re-exported API Surface
 
 These are the compatibility contracts used by JGDMS policy components and by Dirty Chai's authorization implementation.
 
+## Dynamic Grants by ProtectionDomain
+
+The concrete grant classes in `org.apache.river.api.security` (`CertificateGrant`,
+`URIGrant`, `DigestGrant`, `ProtectionDomainGrant`, `ClassLoaderGrant`,
+`PrincipalGrant` and the new `CodebaseDomainGrant`) are package-private and are
+defined by `java.base`. Those copies run on Dirty Chai; JGDMS's copies of the
+same classes are shadowed, so both trees must give the same answers.
+
+* A dynamic grant made through `Security.grant(Class, ...)` uses
+  `PermissionGrantBuilder.CODEBASE_DOMAIN` (`7`, the same value in both trees).
+  It applies to each `ProtectionDomain` of the codebase of the class's
+  `URLClassLoader`, including domains created after the grant, and to no
+  domain outside the codebase. A loader that is not a `URLClassLoader` gets a
+  grant to the class's own `ProtectionDomain` only.
+* `PermissionGrantBuilder.CLASSLOADER` and `ClassLoaderGrant` are deprecated:
+  a grant to a loader as a whole applies to every domain it defines.
+* `CertificateGrant.impliesSigners`: unsigned code never satisfies a grant that
+  requires signers (it used to throw `NullPointerException`); `codeBase` +
+  `signedBy` and `digest` + `signedBy` enforce the signer. A `DigestGrant`
+  ignores the location. An unparseable `codeBase` URI raises
+  `SecurityException`.
+* Code source identity is the location `Uri`, the signers and, for a
+  `DigestCodeSource`, the digest (`CodeSourceKey`).
+* A new grant class must exist in both trees and be built through
+  `PermissionGrantBuilder`: a JGDMS-only subclass of a package-private grant
+  fails with `IllegalAccessError` here.
+
+See `docs/SOW-PermissionGrant-Mirror-and-ProtectionDomain-Grants.md` in JGDMS.
+
 ## Split Package: `org.apache.river.api.security`
 
 The package `org.apache.river.api.security` is intentionally split between `java.base` and the unnamed module (classpath). This is a deliberate design decision, not an oversight.
